#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102014 :tutorial/content "There are several quantities that businesses need to keep track of to ensure that the business remains healthy:\n\n- Revenue: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company gains after selling $x$ units of product.\n- Cost: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company spends to create $x$ units of product.\n- Profit. The difference between revenue and cost. It represents the amount of *additional* money that the business has earned after paying its costs.\n\nBusinesses often strive to maximize revenue and profit. They also often seek to minimize cost. The candidates test gives them a method for doing this. Let's find out how." :tutorial/id #uuid "7d29b308-bacd-5426-b6c8-43b0da8fcd75" :tutorial/math-academy-id 682 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101994 :tutorial/content "Recall that the price function $p(x)$ gives the *price per unit* at which a company sells $x$ units of a good.\n\nThe revenue function $R(x)$ associated with a price function $p(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the cost function $C(x)$ associated with producing $x$ units of a good can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= (fixed cost) + (number of units) \\cdot (cost per unit) \\\\\n&= (fixed cost) + x \\cdot (cost per unit)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quantity $x\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ is sometimes called the **variable cost** since it is the part of the cost function $C(x)$ that is dependent on the number of units $x$ produced by the company.\n\nFinally, if we know the revenue function $R(x)$ and the cost function $C(x)$, then the profit function $P(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = R(x) - C(x)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb6a794d-d60c-5d16-8912-b69af938c892" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4663 :tutorial/title "A Review of Price and Cost Functions"}]}
